1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:04,604
This project helps a company review and approve new suppliers.

2
00:00:04,704 --> 00:00:08,752
AI supports the review. A person makes the decision.

3
00:00:09,567 --> 00:00:13,060
Here is one fictional supplier, Orion Payments.

4
00:00:13,160 --> 00:00:18,061
The reviewer checks policy evidence before deciding whether to approve it.

5
00:00:18,867 --> 00:00:21,293
The reviewer approves this request.

6
00:00:21,393 --> 00:00:26,551
The system saves the approval, the supplier record, and a local notification.

7
00:00:27,867 --> 00:00:32,341
The records are saved, but the workflow has not confirmed completion.

8
00:00:32,441 --> 00:00:37,065
A restart could repeat work unless recovery handles this safely.

9
00:00:37,867 --> 00:00:43,045
Now the demo forcibly stops the worker: the process carrying out the task.

10
00:00:43,145 --> 00:00:46,084
The saved records remain in the database.

11
00:00:46,900 --> 00:00:50,287
A replacement process picks up the same workflow.

12
00:00:50,387 --> 00:00:56,824
Temporal, the workflow engine, retries the interrupted step without asking for
approval again.

13
00:00:57,633 --> 00:01:02,535
Here is the evidence: Temporal shows attempt two, followed by completed.

14
00:01:02,635 --> 00:01:05,275
The interrupted workflow has recovered.

15
00:01:06,633 --> 00:01:12,537
Now check the business records: one approval, one supplier, and one local
notification.

16
00:01:12,637 --> 00:01:16,173
The retry created no duplicates for this case.

17
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:24,952
Temporal remembers the workflow's progress. PostgreSQL prevents the same business
action from being saved twice.

18
00:01:25,052 --> 00:01:28,140
AI advice cannot approve a supplier.

19
00:01:28,967 --> 00:01:34,465
Try it yourself: review a supplier, approve it, and reproduce the crash locally.

20
00:01:34,565 --> 00:01:38,763
The default demonstration needs no paid model credentials.
